WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.647 --> 00:00:06.884
- So my name is Tracie Addy, I work here

00:00:06.884 --> 00:00:08.613
at the Center for Teaching and Learning,

00:00:08.613 --> 00:00:10.725
and I co-direct our
National Summer Institutes

00:00:10.725 --> 00:00:12.404
on scientific teaching.

00:00:12.404 --> 00:00:14.632
I also work with faculty
teaching initiatives

00:00:14.632 --> 00:00:16.671
and this past semester, I had the pleasure

00:00:16.671 --> 00:00:19.237
of teaching a course that was

00:00:19.237 --> 00:00:21.711
Scientific Teaching for Life Sciences

00:00:21.711 --> 00:00:23.439
for graduate students and postdocs.

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:25.148
So what I want to talk about today

00:00:25.148 --> 00:00:27.403
is basically how I piloted,

00:00:27.403 --> 00:00:29.587
I consider it a huge pilot,

00:00:29.587 --> 00:00:32.587
a tool on the peer review in Canvas.

00:00:34.082 --> 00:00:35.604
Okay, so just a little bit of context

00:00:35.604 --> 00:00:37.674
so that you know my course.

00:00:37.674 --> 00:00:39.341
I co-taught BBS 879,

00:00:41.866 --> 00:00:43.844
which is, again, the Theory and Practice

00:00:43.844 --> 00:00:45.961
of Scientific Teaching for Life Sciences.

00:00:45.961 --> 00:00:47.706
The level of this course
was graduate students

00:00:47.706 --> 00:00:49.320
and postdocs, mostly postdocs.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.226
About 16 to four or so graduate students.

00:00:53.226 --> 00:00:54.763
But this tool could be used in any type

00:00:54.763 --> 00:00:57.240
of class, undergraduate et cetera.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.468
So the assignment that my students had

00:00:59.468 --> 00:01:03.052
was to create a diversity statement.

00:01:03.052 --> 00:01:05.167
So, a lot of these
postdocs or grad students

00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:06.709
will be going out to the job market,

00:01:06.709 --> 00:01:09.050
so we have a variety of tools in the class

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:10.429
focused on career development.

00:01:10.429 --> 00:01:13.496
And one of them was creating
a diversity statement.

00:01:13.496 --> 00:01:15.282
So this is the prompt they were given,

00:01:15.282 --> 00:01:18.465
basically to simulate
if they were applying

00:01:18.465 --> 00:01:20.718
to a position and they had to prepare

00:01:20.718 --> 00:01:23.384
this particular statement.

00:01:23.384 --> 00:01:25.709
So why did I use the peer review tool?

00:01:25.709 --> 00:01:28.439
Well essentially, our students being

00:01:28.439 --> 00:01:30.183
postdocs and graduate students,

00:01:30.183 --> 00:01:33.885
a lot of them have this
season of missing classes.

00:01:33.885 --> 00:01:37.991
So they had conferences, they
had a whole bunch of things.

00:01:37.991 --> 00:01:39.154
I think in the month of April,

00:01:39.154 --> 00:01:41.329
we actually Zoomed our class

00:01:41.329 --> 00:01:44.334
so that all of our virtual
students could come in,

00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:47.410
because of all of their many
obligations and whatnot.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.108
And so, what I thought, was because

00:01:49.108 --> 00:01:52.184
we had a two-part session
on diversity statements,

00:01:52.184 --> 00:01:54.214
one being what is a diversity statement,

00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:57.089
the second being you're part
of the hiring committee,

00:01:57.089 --> 00:01:59.508
how can we train you to look at criteria

00:01:59.508 --> 00:02:01.742
to evaluate the diversity statement.

00:02:01.742 --> 00:02:04.960
And to actually do that
peer review in class.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:06.854
I thought it would be great to be able

00:02:06.854 --> 00:02:09.189
to have the students
who weren't able to come

00:02:09.189 --> 00:02:11.321
to actually still do the peer review.

00:02:11.321 --> 00:02:12.531
So this was the rationale.

00:02:12.531 --> 00:02:14.408
So there was a subset of students

00:02:14.408 --> 00:02:17.557
that were to complete this assignment

00:02:17.557 --> 00:02:19.597
online, for helpful reasons.

00:02:19.597 --> 00:02:22.271
It is a non-credit course, but

00:02:22.271 --> 00:02:24.044
they sign up and apply
to take this course,

00:02:24.044 --> 00:02:26.227
so they're usually
pretty curious about it.

00:02:26.227 --> 00:02:29.652
Okay, so this is what
I did for peer review.

00:02:29.652 --> 00:02:32.183
So essentially in our Canvas course site,

00:02:32.183 --> 00:02:33.884
I created an assignment

00:02:33.884 --> 00:02:36.791
that was essentially peer review.

00:02:36.791 --> 00:02:39.140
And I basically told them upload your

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.381
draft of your diversity statement,

00:02:41.381 --> 00:02:43.150
and what I did when I actually

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.617
went in was I paired specific students.

00:02:45.617 --> 00:02:47.551
And you can do this in
Canvas, which is great,

00:02:47.551 --> 00:02:48.933
with other students.

00:02:48.933 --> 00:02:50.471
So everybody was supposed to,

00:02:50.471 --> 00:02:54.146
in class, have two people
review their statement.

00:02:54.146 --> 00:02:56.257
So I set up the same option on Canvas

00:02:56.257 --> 00:02:57.350
for those students who were going

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.183
to be virtual as well.

00:02:59.183 --> 00:03:01.276
Now a couple of neat things that

00:03:01.276 --> 00:03:04.631
I think Canvas can do for peer review

00:03:04.631 --> 00:03:06.182
is that you can actually implement

00:03:06.182 --> 00:03:08.393
a grading rubric inside

00:03:08.393 --> 00:03:10.381
the actual peer review feature.

00:03:10.381 --> 00:03:12.707
So I did this, because actually in class,

00:03:12.707 --> 00:03:14.485
we went over the rubric.

00:03:14.485 --> 00:03:16.246
We went through the hiring committee

00:03:16.246 --> 00:03:19.711
based upon the hiring committee of a

00:03:19.711 --> 00:03:23.167
particular institution out in California.

00:03:23.167 --> 00:03:24.956
So we kind of used that as a model.

00:03:24.956 --> 00:03:27.416
And I created the criteria based

00:03:27.416 --> 00:03:29.156
upon this and to basically plug them

00:03:29.156 --> 00:03:32.216
this into the rubric function and also

00:03:32.216 --> 00:03:34.456
added a couple of other areas there.

00:03:34.456 --> 00:03:36.297
So what the students can do

00:03:36.297 --> 00:03:39.727
when their peer review
person they're paired with

00:03:39.727 --> 00:03:42.520
uploads their particular statement,

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.075
they can actually see their statement.

00:03:44.075 --> 00:03:46.724
So it's a nice window
view of their statement

00:03:46.724 --> 00:03:49.620
as if you have a Microsoft
Word document up.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.835
And then they also show this particular

00:03:51.835 --> 00:03:54.203
rubric, so they can actually edit

00:03:54.203 --> 00:03:56.584
and give comments directly
on their document.

00:03:56.584 --> 00:03:58.032
And they can also give comments

00:03:58.032 --> 00:03:59.431
and feedback on the rubric.

00:03:59.431 --> 00:04:01.896
I was very interested in the rubric per se

00:04:01.896 --> 00:04:03.284
because we wanted to say well, would

00:04:03.284 --> 00:04:05.654
you hire this person based on their

00:04:05.654 --> 00:04:07.295
diversity statement, although
they'd have a whole package,

00:04:07.295 --> 00:04:08.739
but would you actually hire them,

00:04:08.739 --> 00:04:11.585
and so they could actually tally

00:04:11.585 --> 00:04:13.379
the items to figure out if they

00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:15.572
had a high score or not.

00:04:15.572 --> 00:04:17.063
So what were some benefits?

00:04:17.063 --> 00:04:18.821
So, obviously, I talked a little

00:04:18.821 --> 00:04:20.204
bit about well our students were

00:04:20.204 --> 00:04:21.722
not in class, so how could we

00:04:21.722 --> 00:04:24.787
still have them have a similar experience?

00:04:24.787 --> 00:04:26.799
And creating it outside of class

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:30.397
digitally definitely did
this in some ways for them.

00:04:30.397 --> 00:04:32.116
Students can be assigned, as I mentioned,

00:04:32.116 --> 00:04:34.398
specific partners, so
you could pair them up.

00:04:34.398 --> 00:04:36.413
You could also, though,
do this anonymously.

00:04:36.413 --> 00:04:38.707
So you could do anonymous
peer reviews as well.

00:04:38.707 --> 00:04:42.271
Depends on your goals, what
you desire in the course.

00:04:42.271 --> 00:04:44.257
Students can insert comments directly in,

00:04:44.257 --> 00:04:45.912
so I think that's also a bonus.

00:04:45.912 --> 00:04:47.711
I don't know if Canvas
always had that option,

00:04:47.711 --> 00:04:49.445
but I think it's pretty neat to

00:04:49.445 --> 00:04:52.726
actually be able to insert
on the document itself.

00:04:52.726 --> 00:04:56.058
And you can upload rubrics or criteria,

00:04:56.058 --> 00:04:58.479
in any form or fashion.

00:04:58.479 --> 00:05:00.203
Okay, so there were a few challenges

00:05:00.203 --> 00:05:01.888
that I'd like to also express to you.

00:05:01.888 --> 00:05:04.517
This was my first time
using the tool in the class,

00:05:04.517 --> 00:05:07.160
so students, I realized, some of them,

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.366
they needed to kind of
know how to use the tool.

00:05:09.366 --> 00:05:11.428
So I probably should've in the beginning

00:05:11.428 --> 00:05:13.557
showed a few of them them how

00:05:13.557 --> 00:05:16.859
before they went off and
left into all their places.

00:05:16.859 --> 00:05:18.887
But I think I just needed
to give a little bit

00:05:18.887 --> 00:05:20.164
more structure there.

00:05:20.164 --> 00:05:23.324
One of the students was abroad

00:05:23.324 --> 00:05:25.337
and didn't have the proper software.

00:05:25.337 --> 00:05:26.450
I don't know why, I'd have to ask

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:28.755
(mumbles) at another
point, but we just said

00:05:28.755 --> 00:05:30.980
when you come back to
the United States, just

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:32.769
do it then, that's perfectly fine.

00:05:32.769 --> 00:05:34.660
But some of the software,
or whatever happened,

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:35.727
was incompatible.

00:05:35.727 --> 00:05:37.611
And if the peer reviewer's partner didn't

00:05:37.611 --> 00:05:40.737
upload their assignment,
obviously they can't start.

00:05:40.737 --> 00:05:41.570
So I think it's a good idea if you

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:45.245
use the tool to definitely set deadlines.

00:05:45.245 --> 00:05:48.040
We did that, encouraged the
students to actually upload.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:50.131
So they're not always waiting
for their peer reviewer

00:05:50.131 --> 00:05:51.133
to come up.

00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:52.610
The nice thing that happens, is

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:55.051
on the Canvas site, you
can see who's your peer,

00:05:55.051 --> 00:05:55.884
or who you're going to peer review

00:05:55.884 --> 00:05:58.193
if you don't do it anonymously as well.

00:05:58.193 --> 00:06:00.784
And then they can see if that person

00:06:00.784 --> 00:06:03.020
has uploaded their files as well.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:04.888
So that's essentially what I did

00:06:04.888 --> 00:06:06.792
for uploading the peer review tool.

00:06:06.792 --> 00:06:08.881
Id like to do it again
maybe in a larger scale.

00:06:08.881 --> 00:06:10.555
I think there was only about five or six

00:06:10.555 --> 00:06:13.798
students that actually used
it in our complete class.

00:06:13.798 --> 00:06:16.833
But overall, I think it
was valuable for them.

00:06:16.833 --> 00:06:18.061
They still felt like they were

00:06:18.061 --> 00:06:19.404
part of the class, even though

00:06:19.404 --> 00:06:21.309
they weren't physically present.

00:06:21.309 --> 00:06:23.535
And they were still able
to do this process as well.

00:06:23.535 --> 00:06:24.776
I imagine that you can also do it

00:06:24.776 --> 00:06:27.503
if you wanted to have students
outside of class, right?

00:06:27.503 --> 00:06:29.534
So just because you don't
want to do the peer review

00:06:29.534 --> 00:06:31.550
in the class, you can use this tool

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:33.890
as well as an option.

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:37.073
So that you don't have to
devote the class time to it.

00:06:37.073 --> 00:06:38.611
So that's it.

00:06:38.611 --> 00:06:41.194
I'd love to take any questions.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:47.239
- Could you as the instructor see all of

00:06:47.239 --> 00:06:49.774
the comments that the
peers gave one another?

00:06:49.774 --> 00:06:51.029
- Yes, and that's a nice thing too.

00:06:51.029 --> 00:06:52.657
Yes, I could actually see everything

00:06:52.657 --> 00:06:53.762
that they wrote.

00:06:53.762 --> 00:06:57.220
So, that's great, because I can use it,

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:59.390
we didn't grade the actual assignment

00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:01.313
itself, but I can actually go in

00:07:01.313 --> 00:07:03.208
and could actually grade it in

00:07:03.208 --> 00:07:05.194
how well they did their peer review.

00:07:05.194 --> 00:07:07.849
And I could actually
comment upon it too as well.

00:07:07.849 --> 00:07:10.399
So I think it's a really nice feature.

00:07:10.399 --> 00:07:12.684
- [Audience Member] When you
comment, is that in the sidebar

00:07:12.684 --> 00:07:13.735
or in the document?

00:07:13.735 --> 00:07:15.207
- So you can comment directly in

00:07:15.207 --> 00:07:16.808
the document, and then the rubric

00:07:16.808 --> 00:07:18.672
is kind of on the side as well.

00:07:18.672 --> 00:07:19.778
So you can kind of see,

00:07:19.778 --> 00:07:21.568
like I can, when I go
in, I can actually see

00:07:21.568 --> 00:07:24.502
this is what scores they
got on the actual rubric,

00:07:24.502 --> 00:07:26.163
which is not showing right now,

00:07:26.163 --> 00:07:28.359
and then where do they insert comments

00:07:28.359 --> 00:07:30.724
or suggestions in the actual document.

00:07:30.724 --> 00:07:32.807
- [Audience Member] And
that's in assignments too,

00:07:32.807 --> 00:07:34.072
if you (mumbles) a peer thing.

00:07:34.072 --> 00:07:36.010
They upload a Word Doc?

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:38.412
- Oh, on the actual assignments.

00:07:38.412 --> 00:07:39.867
Yeah, so if you do any other type

00:07:39.867 --> 00:07:41.899
of assignment, is it,

00:07:41.899 --> 00:07:42.883
it's pretty much like that.

00:07:42.883 --> 00:07:43.775
Yeah, yeah.

00:07:43.775 --> 00:07:45.893
Yeah, it's pretty similar.

00:07:45.893 --> 00:07:46.726
- [Audience Member]
Can you assign teaching

00:07:46.726 --> 00:07:48.492
fellows the same sort of access?

00:07:48.492 --> 00:07:49.742
- Oh, probably.

00:07:50.799 --> 00:07:51.632
I'm assuming.

00:07:51.632 --> 00:07:52.833
Can teaching fellows, actually at this

00:07:52.833 --> 00:07:54.274
Canvas thing, can teaching fellows

00:07:54.274 --> 00:07:55.701
view your entire course right?

00:07:55.701 --> 00:07:57.610
- [Woman] They have the exact same access

00:07:57.610 --> 00:07:58.683
as an instructor so they would be

00:07:58.683 --> 00:08:00.182
able to see that.

00:08:00.182 --> 00:08:02.038
- [Audience Member] Are there any plans if

00:08:02.038 --> 00:08:05.526
you have undergraduate graders
or learning assistants?

00:08:05.526 --> 00:08:07.355
- [Woman] We do have a role specifically

00:08:07.355 --> 00:08:08.628
for grader, and I'm happy to talk

00:08:08.628 --> 00:08:10.163
about that later on if you'd like

00:08:10.163 --> 00:08:11.330
to contact me.

00:08:14.222 --> 00:08:15.555
- Good question.

00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:18.403
- [Audience Member] You
said there was one person

00:08:18.403 --> 00:08:21.308
who had problems with access.

00:08:21.308 --> 00:08:23.102
Was it in general with Canvas

00:08:23.102 --> 00:08:27.010
or was it before, the user function for,

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:29.257
- The actual functions in Canvas.

00:08:29.257 --> 00:08:31.293
So they couldn't log in to Canvas.

00:08:31.293 --> 00:08:32.629
But again, they were abroad, so

00:08:32.629 --> 00:08:34.499
I don't know the computer access.

00:08:34.499 --> 00:08:35.556
And we thought it would be too

00:08:35.556 --> 00:08:36.831
complicated to, you know,

00:08:36.831 --> 00:08:37.863
so I told them to come back.

00:08:37.863 --> 00:08:39.467
But it was the functions in Canvas,

00:08:39.467 --> 00:08:41.641
so I have no idea why.

00:08:41.641 --> 00:08:42.887
That's a good question.

00:08:42.887 --> 00:08:43.720
- [Audience Member] I'm going to share

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:45.445
some of our experience (mumbles) document

00:08:45.445 --> 00:08:48.310
Global Network Schools
in different countries.

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:50.577
And when our students, or students

00:08:50.577 --> 00:08:53.677
from other business
schools around the world,

00:08:53.677 --> 00:08:56.286
especially if they're
accessing from China,

00:08:56.286 --> 00:08:58.703
the file doesn't always work.

00:09:00.372 --> 00:09:02.133
So that they won't be able to use this.

00:09:02.133 --> 00:09:04.240
- Yeah, I think that was his major issue.

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:06.505
He couldn't open the
actual file in Canvas.

00:09:06.505 --> 00:09:10.005
So he couldn't see his partner's document.

00:09:15.014 --> 00:09:16.681
Any other questions?

00:09:18.314 --> 00:09:19.147
Thank you.

00:09:19.147 --> 00:09:22.147
(audience clapping)

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:28.590
(cheerful tones)

